#sidenote(dy: 1.15em, numbered: false)[#outline(depth: 2)]

= Assignment Overview

From the New York Times to fitness trackers and COVID-19 dashboards to Wordle shares, the world is awash in data and visualization. In this assignment, you will reflect on and take stock of the visualizations that pervade your own life, from the practical and functional to the whimsical and fun. You’ll then analyze and critique these visualizations based on your current design knowledge.



= Part 1: Collection and Curation

In Part 1, take screenshots, photos, and/or videos of visualizations that you encounter in your personal, professional, and academic lives. The goal with this part is to reflect on the pervasiveness of visualizations in your life, observe their use and purpose, and finally analyze them. While you will certainly include some examples of “standard” visualizations like barcharts and the like, the best assignments will be able to also construe “data visualization” broadly. Really think about what are some different ways in which data is presented to you graphically for the purpose of sensemaking?

_*Collect a minimum of ten visualizations*_ and organize them in a PDF slideshow (see Deliverables). For each visualization, enter a brief description and how it relates to your life. Make sure to include a citation to the source of the visualization if possible.

The examples should be grouped, ordered, or otherwise organized by some sensible approach that is up to you: you could organize them by chart type, by domain (health, schoolwork, finances, fun, etc.), by communicative purpose (to inform, to persuade,to explore), etc.

= Part 2: Critique

From your collection, *_select three visualizations to critique and analyze_*. For each of the three, address the following questions:

    + What is the goal of the visualization? What do you learn from it?
    + How is the data represented? How do the various graphical marks and visual elements represent the data being shown? This should be directly annotated.
    + What are some strengths and weaknesses of the visualization? In what ways does it do a good job? How can it be improved?
    + What is the historical or cultural context from which this design comes from? Are there any important similarities between this visualization and other earlier visualizations that it is drawing from?




= Part 3: Reflection

Finally you must include a short, 1-2 paragraph learning reflection. You should address questions such as "In this assignment, I learned about… I had not previously considered… I was surprised that…"

